# iOS Build And Environments - {{ project_name }}

## Tooling

- XcodeGen generates the Xcode project from `project.yml`
- Taskfile provides `mobile-ios:generate-project`, `mobile-ios:build`, `mobile-ios:test`, `mobile-ios:test-unit`, and `mobile-ios:test-ui`
- iOS tasks are Mac-only and are guarded with `platforms: [darwin]`
- Only the build and test tasks resolve a simulator ID before calling `xcodebuild`: the available device whose name exactly matches `SIMULATOR_NAME` before the UUID, or the newest available iPhone when `SIMULATOR_NAME` is not set
- When several simulators match, the lookup uses the last one `simctl` reports, which is typically the newest installed runtime
- Release automation lives in `fastlane/Fastfile` and CI, with `bundle exec fastlane beta` as the TestFlight-oriented path

## Generated Project Settings

- Deployment target: iOS 17.0
- Swift version: 6.0
- Xcode version: 26.0
- Default bundle identifier: `{{ package_identifier }}`
- Debug runtime base URL: `http://localhost:8080/api/v1`
- Release runtime base URL placeholder: `https://api.example.com/api/v1`

## Commands

```bash
task mobile-ios:generate-project
task mobile-ios:build
task mobile-ios:test
task mobile-ios:test-unit
task mobile-ios:test-ui
bundle exec fastlane beta
```

Equivalent direct build commands come from the Taskfile and use:

- `xcodegen generate`
- `xcodebuild build -project "{{ project_name }}.xcodeproj" -scheme "{{ ios_module_name }}" -destination "platform=iOS Simulator,id=<resolved simulator id>" -configuration Debug`
- `xcodebuild test -project "{{ project_name }}.xcodeproj" -scheme "{{ ios_module_name }}" -destination "platform=iOS Simulator,id=<resolved simulator id>"`
- `xcodebuild test -project "{{ project_name }}.xcodeproj" -scheme "{{ ios_module_name }}" -destination "platform=iOS Simulator,id=<resolved simulator id>" -only-testing:"{{ ios_module_name }}Tests"`
- `xcodebuild test -project "{{ project_name }}.xcodeproj" -scheme "{{ ios_module_name }}" -destination "platform=iOS Simulator,id=<resolved simulator id>" -only-testing:"{{ ios_module_name }}UITests"`
- `xcodebuild archive -project "{{ project_name }}.xcodeproj" -scheme "{{ ios_module_name }}" -configuration Release -archivePath build/{{ ios_module_name }}.xcarchive`
- `bundle exec fastlane beta`

You can override the simulator lookup when needed:

- `SIMULATOR_NAME="iPhone 17" task mobile-ios:build`
- `SIMULATOR_NAME="iPhone 17" task mobile-ios:test`
- `SIMULATOR_NAME="iPhone 17" task mobile-ios:test-ui`

If no available simulator matches `SIMULATOR_NAME`, or no iPhone simulator is available when it is not set, the build or test task exits with a lookup error before invoking `xcodebuild`. The `fastlane test` lane picks its device the same way.

The generated project keeps `{{ project_slug }}` for filesystem paths and uses `{{ ios_module_name }}` for the Swift/Xcode target and scheme.

When `project.yml` changes structure rather than just source contents, regenerate before building. Typical triggers include new targets or schemes, Swift Packages, xcconfig wiring, entitlements, capabilities, resource bundles, or deployment-target changes.

## UI Test Mode

- The app includes a lightweight UI-test launch mode so generated XCUITests can verify flows without a live backend.
- Generated UI tests launch the app with `UI_TEST_MODE=1` and an auth-state override such as `UI_TEST_AUTH_STATE=authenticated`.
- In UI-test mode, the app swaps in deterministic repositories and seeds session state when requested.
- Preserve stable accessibility identifiers on screen roots and key controls when changing UI that is covered by XCUITests.
- The generated UI suite is intentionally smoke-level today; password-form automation is a known follow-up once SwiftUI exposes those controls more reliably to XCUITest.

## Non-Mac Fallback

- If you are not on Mac, you cannot run the generated iOS build or test tasks locally.
- In that case, explicitly report which verification steps were skipped and defer real iOS validation to Mac CI or a Mac follow-up session.

## Runtime Configuration

- `project.yml` wires `Config/Debug.xcconfig` and `Config/Release.xcconfig` through XcodeGen `configFiles`.
- Both files are git-ignored local files. The tracked `Config/Debug.xcconfig.example` and `Config/Release.xcconfig.example` are their starting point.
- On a fresh clone or in CI, create the local files before `xcodegen generate`: `cp Config/Debug.xcconfig.example Config/Debug.xcconfig` and `cp Config/Release.xcconfig.example Config/Release.xcconfig`. `task mobile-ios:generate-project` does this when a file is missing.
- XcodeGen fails on a missing config file, so the generated CI workflow creates them in a step before `xcodegen generate`.
- xcconfig treats `//` as the start of a comment. Write URLs as `http:/$()/host/path`; the empty `$()` keeps the scheme separator from being cut off.
- `Info.plist` reads `$(API_BASE_URL)`.
- `APIClient` defaults to the Info.plist value and falls back to `http://localhost:8080/api/v1` if the value is missing or empty. It joins the base URL and each endpoint path with exactly one `/`, and a base URL without a scheme or host fails the request as an invalid URL.
- Update `Config/Release.xcconfig` before building a production or TestFlight release. CI creates that file from `Config/Release.xcconfig.example`, so set the production URL there as well before tagging a release.

## Current Scope

- The template scaffolds a single app target plus unit-test and UI-test targets.
- It does not scaffold multi-environment schemes or device-install workflows by default.
